Here's how to save and restore draft text using Window lifecycle events:

## Custom Window with State Preservation

```csharp
// App.xaml.cs
public partial class App : Application
{
    protected override Window CreateWindow(IActivationState? activationState)
        => new AppWindow(new AppShell());
}

// AppWindow.cs
public class AppWindow : Window
{
    public AppWindow(Page page) : base(page) { }

    protected override void OnStopped()
    {
        base.OnStopped();
        SaveDraft();
    }

    protected override void OnDestroying()
    {
        base.OnDestroying();
        SaveDraft(); // Android back-button can skip Stopped
    }

    protected override void OnResumed()
    {
        base.OnResumed();
        if (GetViewModel() is NoteViewModel vm)
            vm.DraftText = Preferences.Get("draft_text", string.Empty);
    }

    private void SaveDraft()
    {
        if (GetViewModel() is NoteViewModel vm)
            Preferences.Set("draft_text", vm.DraftText);
    }

    private NoteViewModel? GetViewModel()
        => (Shell.Current?.CurrentPage as NotePage)?.BindingContext as NoteViewModel;
}
```

## ViewModel

```csharp
public partial class NoteViewModel : ObservableObject
{
    [ObservableProperty]
    private string draftText = Preferences.Get("draft_text", string.Empty);
}
```

## Key Points

| Concern | Approach |
|---------|----------|
| Save on background | `OnStopped` — app is no longer visible |
| Save on Android back | `OnDestroying` — back button can skip `Stopped` |
| Restore on resume | `OnResumed` — fires when returning from background |
| First launch restore | Load in ViewModel constructor via `Preferences.Get` |
| Keep handlers fast | `Preferences` is lightweight; avoid DB writes here |

**Important:** `OnResumed` does *not* fire on first launch (only `Created` → `Activated`), so load the initial draft in the ViewModel constructor. Keep lifecycle handlers under 1–2 seconds to avoid ANR on Android or watchdog kills on iOS.
